#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cd176d1-71e6-5d2d-a259-a3fcc0af991f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266962} {:answer/id #uuid "62df8b67-d01e-577d-9807-c0b20a784a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267013} {:answer/id #uuid "9c8b3ad7-9956-5469-b111-e37bf7bc1bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267549} {:answer/id #uuid "b0142210-122c-5cb4-9c32-769b018c4a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267729} {:answer/id #uuid "dcebd8e5-4a98-5a88-b29f-0e1f28a253c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0142210-122c-5cb4-9c32-769b018c4a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267729} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9c4369a-8e31-5a0a-8b79-02cda6e1bfbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86de2721-fbfb-53fc-8404-fca3429d9ecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132601" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}y+2x-y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{y}(-2,2)(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2y-1$ (or $2y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $j+2\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1f69732-5e50-5b65-89ef-95823d50c6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y = 1,; z = 4x - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122457} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d284c3-b75c-5802-a424-0e940b3ffd75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y = 1; z = 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122458} {:answer/id #uuid "32364905-605a-5bf2-913a-acf3e415ffa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y = 1; z = 2x - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122459} {:answer/id #uuid "782ea77e-69d2-5c59-b771-1bad28e70e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y = 1; z = 2 - 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122460} {:answer/id #uuid "7fcf1b4e-725d-5976-aa0e-41725cf3a02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y = 1; z = 3x - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122461}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c9c1faf1-2d7b-5e78-a408-90a8cb93ac6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122456} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d90446d-468e-5505-8c08-1f46b0193618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2-2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385352} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f459ee-dee1-5d31-a4d4-71a204434fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1, \\\\ z=4x-4\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385353} {:answer/id #uuid "7621cca1-35e1-500f-b716-2d3e51c1d38e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385354} {:answer/id #uuid "c43b040e-4e33-5989-9fb5-766a3deff3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385355} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f78f87-4d49-51d8-924b-bd1e64d65e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c43b040e-4e33-5989-9fb5-766a3deff3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385355} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec17033d-7e32-53b3-b00f-f4c5e370a468" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "faf15e19-a012-50bb-842f-791131393cd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36369" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3x+6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,1,0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)} & =3(1)+6(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =3+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)}(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =3(x-1) \\\\ z & =3x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022e71c7-1d96-5b12-ab9b-3c65246aca6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499657} {:answer/id #uuid "4ecbc247-a7b4-51ad-ac21-7f50b73d7f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499803} {:answer/id #uuid "4f28db71-8536-543c-a65e-9e2f2a45d22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499806} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf544a9-31d0-5386-9054-024015e9e1e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=3x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500070} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fec6bc-c021-533e-ab85-5127fbbe71f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=2x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f28db71-8536-543c-a65e-9e2f2a45d22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499806} :answer-field/id #uuid "1450b1c6-e44c-5c3f-b403-2b991fbf5f5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac629eca-77ed-5bb7-99e7-49845bdc5d9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71204" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=0.$ Given that the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(y+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},0)\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)} & =(0+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =1(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7b7abb-15fc-5e25-af71-ce99a58976f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425091} {:answer/id #uuid "14e7d503-39ad-5577-a641-1da1d8c70d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425094} {:answer/id #uuid "348a1560-eed6-553f-a07c-d5dbb0b13eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425098} {:answer/id #uuid "69c34c73-ac38-5363-8c2d-999f5d258274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425103} {:answer/id #uuid "759cdd15-f659-5676-8d91-9b242c2d2b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "348a1560-eed6-553f-a07c-d5dbb0b13eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425098} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7802a6-4174-5690-9124-8f551b3466e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "666541af-0f77-5a3b-95c7-e3f70c0a6b57" :question/math-academy-id "q-71228" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)-(x+y)^{3}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)-(1+0)^{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,0) & ={f}_{y}(1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =-1(y) \\\\ z+1 & =-y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-1$ (or $y+z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $-j+k.$"}]}
